sections.wikitext:
"Boulevard of Broken Dreams" was named Record of the Year at the [[48th Grammy Awards|Grammy Awards of 2006]]. The song's broad appeal was demonstrated by its performance on several ''[[Billboard (magazine)|Billboard]]'' singles charts: it spent 14 weeks at number one on the [[Hot Mainstream Rock Tracks|Mainstream Rock Tracks]] chart staying there for 38 weeks, 16 weeks at number one on the [[Alternative Songs|Modern Rock Tracks]] chart staying for 32 weeks, 11 weeks at number one on the [[Adult Top 40]] chart staying at 44 weeks, and four weeks at number one on the [[Mainstream Top 40]] staying there for 26 weeks. This was the first song to top the four charts altogether making this song a multi-chart success. On the issue dated March 5, 2005, it reached number two on the ''Billboard'' Hot 100 (the highest a Green Day song has ever charted on the Billboard Hot 100), staying there for five weeks behind [[50 Cent]]'s "[[Candy Shop]]". This was also the first Green Day song to reach the [[Adult Contemporary (chart)|Adult Contemporary]] chart, peaking at number 30 and though "[[Good Riddance (Time of Your Life)]]" didn't chart on the Adult Contemporary, it did chart on its recurrent chart.{{cn|date=October 2024}} Throughout 2005, it sold 803,790 digital copies in the United States.<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://drownedinsound.com/news/551425-geeks-win-weezer-is-biggest-download-of-2005 |title=Geeks win! =Weezer= is biggest download of 2005 / Music News // Drowned In Sound |access-date=February 13, 2026 |archive-date=March 18, 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250318141836/https://drownedinsound.com/news/551425-geeks-win-weezer-is-biggest-download-of-2005 |url-status=dead }}</ref>

Outside the United States, "Boulevard of Broken Dreams" charted strongly on many international charts. The song debuted and peaked at number five on the [[UK Singles Chart]] on the chart dated December 5, 2004, giving the band their third top 10 single in that country. It stayed in the UK top 100 for 29 weeks, becoming their longest-charting single at the time, but "[[Wake Me Up When September Ends]]" would log 32 weeks in the UK top 100 nearly a year later; it remains their second-longest stay on the UK chart.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.officialcharts.com/artist/17908/green-day/|title=Green Day Chart History|publisher=[[Official Charts Company]]|access-date=July 20, 2018|archive-date=July 8, 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220708204434/https://www.officialcharts.com/artist/17908/green-day/|url-status=live}}</ref> In 2021, the [[British Phonographic Industry]] awarded the song a Platinum certification for sales and streams exceeding 600,000 units. The single was successful in Ireland, reaching number two in January 2005 on two occasions and totaling 23 weeks in the top 50.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.chart-track.co.uk/index.jsp?c=p%2Fmusicvideo%2Fmusic%2Farchive%2Findex_test.jsp&ct=240001&arch=t&lyr=2005&year=2005&week=18|title=Top 50 Singles, Week Ending 5 May 2005|access-date=July 20, 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180720225111/https://www.chart-track.co.uk/index.jsp?c=p%2Fmusicvideo%2Fmusic%2Farchive%2Findex_test.jsp&ct=240001&arch=t&lyr=2005&year=2005&week=18|archive-date=July 20, 2018|url-status=dead}}</ref> It was their highest-peaking single there until "[[The Saints Are Coming#U2 and Green Day cover|The Saints Are Coming]]" topped the [[Irish Singles Chart]] in 2006. Elsewhere in Europe, the song reached number one in the Czech Republic, number two in Sweden, and the top 10 in Austria, Denmark, and Norway. In [[Australasia]], the song reached number five in both Australia and New Zealand. It stayed on the latter country's chart longer, remaining on the [[Recorded Music NZ|RIANZ]] chart for 25 weeks compared to 17 weeks on the [[ARIA Charts|ARIA Singles Chart]]. Despite this, the song was overall more popular in Australia, finishing 2005 as the 31st best-selling single and earning a Platinum certification from the [[Australian Recording Industry Association|ARIA]] for sales exceeding 70,000 copies.{{cn|date=October 2024}}

In response to [[Hurricane Katrina]] and the popularity of "Boulevard of Broken Dreams", Green Day donated all of the [[iTunes]] proceeds from this song for the year to the [[American Red Cross]] for Katrina aid efforts.{{cn|date=October 2024}}

